// LeetCode #319: Bulb Switcher — the closed form, via Newton's integer sqrt.
//
// `n` bulbs start off. Round 1 toggles every bulb, round 2 every second bulb,
// round `i` every `i`-th bulb, up to round `n`. How many are on at the end?
//
// Bulb `b` is toggled once per DIVISOR of `b`, so it ends on exactly when `b`
// has an odd number of divisors. Divisors come in pairs `(d, b/d)`, and the
// only way a pair fails to be two distinct numbers is `d == b/d` — that is,
// `b` is a perfect square. So the bulbs left on are precisely the squares
// `1, 4, 9, …` not exceeding `n`, and the answer is `floor(sqrt(n))`.
//
// That leaves one real problem: computing an integer square root without ever
// touching a float, because `(i64) sqrt((double) n)` is wrong near the top of
// the range — a double has 53 bits of mantissa and an i64 has 63, so beyond
// 2^53 the conversion rounds and the result can land one off. Newton's method
// on integers has no such window. `x -> (x + n/x) / 2` converges downward from
// any overestimate and, in integer arithmetic, settles exactly on
// `floor(sqrt(n))`; the loop stops on the first step that fails to decrease.
//
// Overflow: the first iteration computes `n + n/n = n + 1`, which is the
// largest value this ever forms, so every `n` below `i64::MAX` is safe.
// Afterwards `x` only shrinks and `x + n/x` stays near `2*sqrt(n)`.
//
// Only half of the `n < 2` guard is load-bearing, and it is worth saying which:
// `n == 0` must not reach `n / x`, because the first `x` is `n`. `n == 1` needs
// no guard at all — the loop computes `y = (1 + 1) / 2 = 1`, fails to decrease,
// and returns 1 on its own. Weakening the guard to `n < 1` is a mutation the
// differential cannot kill (M1 in the README's table), which is the evidence
// for that claim rather than an argument for making the change: `n < 2` states
// "0 and 1 are their own square roots", which is the property a reader wants.

fn isqrt(n: i64) -> i64 {
    if n < 2 { return n; }
    let mut x = n;
    loop {
        let y = (x + n / x) / 2;
        if y >= x { break; }
        x = y;
    }
    return x;
}

fn bulb_switch(n: i64) -> i64 {
    return isqrt(n);
}

fn main() {
    let cases: Vec[i64] = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 15, 16, 99, 100, 101,
                           999999, 1000000, 1000000000, 4503599761588224];
    for i in 0..cases.len() {
        let n = cases[i];
        print("n = " + n.to_string() + " -> " + bulb_switch(n).to_string() + "\n");
    }
}
